@chapter FORTH Frontend Language
@cindex FORTH frontend language

This chapter describes the FORTH frontend as implemented in the EightBol compiler.
The FORTH frontend provides a stack-based, concatenative programming model for
creating compiled applications targeting 8-bit and 16-bit microprocessors. FORTH
programs are compiled to the EIGHTBOL Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) and then to
assembly language for processors including the 6502, 65c02, 65c816, Z80, and others.

@section Overview

FORTH is a stack-based, concatenative language where programs consist of words
(subroutines) that manipulate a parameter stack. The FORTH frontend in EIGHTBOL
provides a comprehensive implementation supporting classic FORTH operations as well
as domain-specific extensions for dialogue, narrative, and interactive fiction.

The frontend handles:
@itemize
@item Stack operations (DUP, SWAP, DROP, OVER, ROT, 2DUP, 2SWAP, 2DROP)
@item Arithmetic operations (+, -, *, /, MOD, ABS, NEGATE)
@item Comparison operations (=, <, >, <=, >=, <>)
@item Bitwise operations (AND, OR, XOR, NOT, <<, >>)
@item Memory access (@ fetch, ! store, +! add-store, C@ byte-fetch, C! byte-store)
@item Return stack operations (>R, R>, R@)
@item Input/output (., ."", EMIT, CR, SPACE, SPACES, PAGE, KEY, ACCEPT)
@item Control flow (IF/THEN/ELSE, BEGIN/UNTIL, BEGIN/WHILE, DO/LOOP, DO/+LOOP)
@item Variables and constants (VARIABLE, CONSTANT)
@item Word definitions (: ... ;)
@item Multiple number formats (decimal, hexadecimal, octal, binary, dword)
@item Dialogue and narrative extensions (SAY, NARRATE, SHOW, HIDE, FADE, MUSIC, SOUND)
@end itemize

@section Lexical Elements

The FORTH frontend tokenizes source code into the following categories:

@subsection Stack Operation Words
Stack operations manipulate the parameter stack without performing computation:

@table @asis
@item @code{DUP}
Duplicate top of stack. Stack effect: (x -- x x)

@item @code{SWAP}
Exchange top two stack items. Stack effect: (x y -- y x)

@item @code{DROP}
Discard top of stack. Stack effect: (x -- )

@item @code{OVER}
Copy second item to top. Stack effect: (x y -- x y x)

@item @code{ROT}
Rotate top three items. Stack effect: (x y z -- y z x)

@item @code{2DUP}
Duplicate top two items. Stack effect: (x y -- x y x y)

@item @code{2SWAP}
Swap top two pairs. Stack effect: (x1 x2 y1 y2 -- y1 y2 x1 x2)

@item @code{2DROP}
Discard top two items. Stack effect: (x y -- )
@end table

@subsection Arithmetic Words
Arithmetic words perform calculations on stack values:

@table @asis
@item @code{+}
Addition. Stack effect: (x y -- x+y)

@item @code{-}
Subtraction. Stack effect: (x y -- x-y)

@item @code{*}
Multiplication. Stack effect: (x y -- x*y)

@item @code{/}
Division. Stack effect: (x y -- x/y)

@item @code{MOD}
Modulo (remainder). Stack effect: (x y -- x mod y)

@item @code{ABS}
Absolute value. Stack effect: (x -- |x|)

@item @code{NEGATE}
Negate (unary minus). Stack effect: (x -- -x)

@item @code{1+}
Increment by 1. Stack effect: (x -- x+1)

@item @code{1-}
Decrement by 1. Stack effect: (x -- x-1)

@item @code{2*}
Multiply by 2 (left shift). Stack effect: (x -- 2x)

@item @code{2/}
Divide by 2 (right shift). Stack effect: (x -- x/2)
@end table

@subsection Comparison Words
Comparison words test relationships between stack values:

@table @asis
@item @code{=}
Equal. Stack effect: (x y -- f) where f is true/false

@item @code{<}
Less than. Stack effect: (x y -- f)

@item @code{>}
Greater than. Stack effect: (x y -- f)

@item @code{<=}
Less than or equal. Stack effect: (x y -- f)

@item @code{>=}
Greater than or equal. Stack effect: (x y -- f)

@item @code{<>}
Not equal. Stack effect: (x y -- f)

@item @code{0=}
Zero test. Stack effect: (x -- f)

@item @code{0<}
Negative test. Stack effect: (x -- f)

@item @code{0>}
Positive test. Stack effect: (x -- f)
@end table

@subsection Bitwise Words
Bitwise words manipulate individual bits in stack values:

@table @asis
@item @code{AND}
Bitwise AND. Stack effect: (x y -- x&y)

@item @code{OR}
Bitwise OR. Stack effect: (x y -- x|y)

@item @code{XOR}
Bitwise XOR. Stack effect: (x y -- x^y)

@item @code{NOT}
Bitwise NOT (complement). Stack effect: (x -- ~x)

@item @code{<<}
Left shift (logical). Stack effect: (x n -- x<<n)

@item @code{>>}
Right shift (logical). Stack effect: (x n -- x>>n)
@end table

@subsection Memory Access Words
Memory access words read from and write to system memory:

@table @asis
@item @code{@}
Fetch (read) from memory at address. Stack effect: (addr -- value)

@item @code{!}
Store (write) to memory at address. Stack effect: (value addr -- )

@item @code{+!}
Add to memory location. Stack effect: (incr addr -- )

@item @code{C@}
Fetch byte from memory. Stack effect: (addr -- byte)

@item @code{C!}
Store byte to memory. Stack effect: (byte addr -- )
@end table

@subsection Return Stack Words
Return stack words access the return stack used for control flow:

@table @asis
@item @code{>R}
Push to return stack. Stack effect: (x -- ) R: ( -- x)

@item @code{R>}
Pop from return stack. Stack effect: ( -- x) R: (x -- )

@item @code{R@}
Copy return stack top (read without popping). Stack effect: ( -- x) R: (x -- x)
@end table

@subsection Input/Output Words
Input/output words handle communication with the user or display:

@table @asis
@item @code{.}
Print top of stack as decimal number. Stack effect: (x -- )

@item @code{."} text @code{"}
Print string literal. Stack effect: ( -- )

@item @code{EMIT}
Output a single character (by ASCII/character code). Stack effect: (char -- )

@item @code{CR}
Output carriage return (newline). Stack effect: ( -- )

@item @code{SPACE}
Output a single space character. Stack effect: ( -- )

@item @code{SPACES}
Output N space characters. Stack effect: (n -- )

@item @code{PAGE}
Clear screen/output buffer. Stack effect: ( -- )

@item @code{KEY}
Read a single keystroke. Stack effect: ( -- char)

@item @code{ACCEPT}
Read a line of text from input. Stack effect: (addr len -- count)
@end table

@subsection Control Flow Words
Control flow words direct program execution:

@table @asis
@item @code{IF} ... @code{THEN} ... @code{ELSE} ... @code{ENDIF}
Conditional execution. If top of stack is true (non-zero), execute THEN clause; 
otherwise execute ELSE clause. Stack effect: (f -- )

@item @code{BEGIN} ... @code{UNTIL}
Loop until condition is true. Stack effect: (repeated)

@item @code{BEGIN} ... @code{WHILE} ... @code{REPEAT}
Loop while condition is true. Stack effect: (repeated)

@item @code{DO} ... @code{LOOP}
Counted loop with implicit counter. Stack effect: (start end -- )

@item @code{DO} ... @code{+LOOP}
Counted loop with custom increment. Stack effect: (start end -- )

@item @code{I}
Fetch current loop counter (inside DO loops). Stack effect: ( -- n)

@item @code{J}
Fetch outer loop counter (for nested loops). Stack effect: ( -- n)

@item @code{RECURSE}
Recursive call to current word definition (tail-call optimization applied).
@end table

@subsection Variable and Constant Words
Variable and constant words create named storage:

@table @asis
@item @code{VARIABLE} name
Create a named variable. Example: @code{VARIABLE counter}

@item @code{CONSTANT} name
Create a named constant. Example: @code{42 CONSTANT ANSWER}

@item @code{VALUE} name
Create a named value (like constant but with @code{TO name} assignment).
@end table

@subsection Dialogue and Narrative Extensions
FORTH dialect extensions enable narrative and dialogue functionality:

@table @asis
@item @code{SAY} "text"
Output dialogue text. Stack effect: ( -- )

@item @code{NARRATE} "text"
Output narrative text. Stack effect: ( -- )

@item @code{SHOW} sprite-name
Display sprite or visual element. Stack effect: ( -- )

@item @code{HIDE} sprite-name
Hide sprite or visual element. Stack effect: ( -- )

@item @code{FADE} direction
Fade in or out (e.g., @code{FADE out}, @code{FADE in}). Stack effect: ( -- )

@item @code{MUSIC} "filename"
Play background music. Stack effect: ( -- )

@item @code{SOUND} "filename"
Play sound effect. Stack effect: ( -- )
@end table

@section Number Literal Formats

The FORTH frontend supports multiple number literal formats:

@table @asis
@item Decimal
Standard base-10 numbers. Examples: @code{42}, @code{-123}, @code{0}

@item Hexadecimal
Base-16 numbers prefixed with @code{$} or @code{0x}. 
Examples: @code{$FF}, @code{$DEAD}, @code{0xDEADBEEF}

@item Octal
Base-8 numbers prefixed with @code{0o}. 
Examples: @code{0o777}, @code{0o123}

@item Binary
Base-2 numbers prefixed with @code{%} or @code{0b}. 
Examples: @code{%1010}, @code{%11111111}, @code{0b10101010}

@item Dword (Character Literal)
Four-character literals prefixed with @code{d'} and enclosed in single quotes.
Examples: @code{d'CHAR'}, @code{d'TEST'}, @code{d'    '} (space-padded)
@end table

@section Identifiers and Comments

Identifiers are sequences of alphanumeric characters, hyphens, and underscores.
By convention, FORTH identifiers are uppercase, though the frontend normalizes 
identifiers to uppercase automatically:

@table @asis
@item @code{counter} → @code{COUNTER}
@item @code{my-word} → @code{MY_WORD}
@item @code{_private} → @code{_PRIVATE}
@end table

Comments in FORTH use two formats:

@table @asis
@item Line comments
Backslash (@code{\}) introduces a comment that extends to end of line.
Example: @code{DUP SWAP \ Duplicate and swap}

@item Parenthetical comments
Parentheses delimit comments: @code{( comment text )}
Example: @code{DUP ( x -- x x ) SWAP}
@end table

@section String Literals

Strings are delimited by double quotes and can be used in output statements:

@example
." Hello, World!"
." This is line 1"
SAY "Greetings, brave adventurer!"
@end example

String escape sequences are supported:
@itemize
@item @code{\n} — Newline
@item @code{\t} — Horizontal tab
@item @code{\r} — Carriage return
@item @code{\\} — Literal backslash
@item @code{\"} — Literal double quote
@end itemize

@section Word Definitions

Words (subroutines) are defined using colon definitions:

@example
: word-name
  definition... ;
@end example

Word definitions can reference other words and can be recursive:

@example
: DOUBLE
  DUP + ;

: SQUARE
  DUP * ;

: FACTORIAL
  DUP 1 > IF
    DUP 1 - RECURSE *
  THEN ;
@end example

Within word definitions, @code{RECURSE} provides tail-call optimization for 
recursive definitions.

@section Variables and State

Variables are created with @code{VARIABLE} and accessed with @code{@@} (fetch) 
and @code{!} (store):

@example
VARIABLE counter
10 counter !           \ Store 10
counter @ .            \ Fetch and print
counter @ 1 + counter ! \ Increment
@end example

Constants are similar but immutable:

@example
42 CONSTANT ANSWER
100 CONSTANT WIDTH
ANSWER .               \ Prints: 42
@end example

@section Control Flow Structures

@subsection Conditional Execution

@example
: ABS-VALUE
  DUP 0< IF
    NEGATE
  THEN ;
@end example

@subsection Loops

Begin-Until loops continue until the condition is true:

@example
: COUNT-TO
  BEGIN
    DUP .
    1 +
    DUP 10 >= UNTIL ;
@end example

Begin-While loops continue while the condition is true:

@example
: WHILE-COUNT
  BEGIN
    DUP 10 < WHILE
    DUP . 1 +
  REPEAT ;
@end example

Do loops are counted loops with implicit counter:

@example
: PRINT-1-TO-10
  10 0 DO
    I . CR
  LOOP ;
@end example

@section Abstract Syntax Tree Mapping

FORTH programs are parsed into EIGHTBOL AST nodes:

@table @asis
@item @code{:forth-program}
Top-level program containing word definitions and statements

@item @code{:forth-push-literal}
Push numeric or string literal to stack

@item @code{:forth-push-identifier}
Push variable value to stack

@item @code{:forth-stack-op}
Stack manipulation operation (DUP, SWAP, DROP, etc.)

@item @code{:forth-arithmetic}
Arithmetic operation (+, -, *, /, MOD, ABS, NEGATE)

@item @code{:forth-comparison}
Comparison operation (=, <, >, <=, >=, <>)

@item @code{:forth-bitwise}
Bitwise operation (AND, OR, XOR, NOT, <<, >>)

@item @code{:forth-fetch}
Memory fetch operation (@)

@item @code{:forth-store}
Memory store operation (! or +!)

@item @code{:forth-conditional}
Conditional execution (IF/THEN/ELSE)

@item @code{:forth-loop}
Loop structure (BEGIN/UNTIL, BEGIN/WHILE, DO/LOOP)

@item @code{:forth-input-key}
Read keystroke (KEY)

@item @code{:forth-input-line}
Read line of text (ACCEPT)

@item @code{:forth-print-value}
Print numeric value (.)

@item @code{:forth-print-string}
Print string literal (.")

@item @code{:forth-emit}
Output single character (EMIT)

@item @code{:forth-print-cr}
Output newline (CR)

@item @code{:forth-dialogue}
Output narrative or dialogue text (SAY, NARRATE)

@item @code{:forth-word-def}
Word definition (:...;)

@item @code{:forth-constant-def}
Constant definition (CONSTANT)

@item @code{:forth-variable-def}
Variable definition (VARIABLE)
@end table

@section Example Programs

@subsection Factorial Calculation

@example
: FACTORIAL
  DUP 1 > IF
    DUP 1 - RECURSE *
  ELSE
    DROP 1
  THEN ;

5 FACTORIAL . CR
@end example

@subsection String Output with Dialogue

@example
SAY "Welcome to the adventure!"
CR
NARRATE "You find yourself in a dark forest."
CR
MUSIC "dark-forest.mus"
SOUND "wind-howl.snd"
@end example

@subsection Loop with Counter

@example
: LOOP-EXAMPLE
  BEGIN
    DUP . CR
    1 +
    DUP 10 = UNTIL
  DROP ;

0 LOOP-EXAMPLE
@end example

@subsection Memory Access

@example
VARIABLE buffer

65 buffer !            \ Store ASCII 'A'
buffer @ EMIT CR       \ Output 'A'
buffer @ 1 + buffer !  \ Increment stored value
buffer @ .             \ Print new value
@end example

@section Implementation Notes

@subsection Tokenization

The FORTH lexer tokenizes source into:
@itemize
@item Numeric literals (in multiple bases)
@item String literals (quoted text)
@item Words (identifiers and keywords)
@item Comments (line and parenthetical)
@end itemize

@subsection Identifier Normalization

All identifiers are automatically normalized to uppercase for consistency
with FORTH convention and case-insensitive semantics.

@subsection Type System

FORTH is dynamically typed at runtime. The compiler infers types from context:
@itemize
@item Stack values are generic word-sized entities
@item Memory locations can store arbitrary values
@item Comparison operations produce boolean results
@item Arithmetic operations maintain precision based on backend
@end itemize

@subsection Return Stack Implementation

The return stack is implemented as a separate storage area:
@itemize
@item @code{>R} pushes data stack top to return stack
@item @code{R>} pops return stack top to data stack
@item @code{R@} copies return stack top to data stack (non-destructive)
@end itemize

@section Limitations and Unsupported Features

The following FORTH features are not currently supported:

@itemize
@item Floating-point arithmetic (only integer operations supported)
@item Structures and records (beyond simple variables)
@item File I/O beyond program input/output
@item Advanced control structures (LEAVE, AGAIN)
@item Immediate words and compile-only words
@item Dictionary inspection at runtime
@item Vectored execution
@item Deferred words
@end itemize

@section Backend Code Generation

FORTH source is parsed to AST and then compiled to target assembly:

@itemize
@item 6502/65c02/65c816: Accumulator-based code generation
@item Z80/HuC6280: Specialized code generation for each processor
@item Stack VM: Direct stack machine code
@end itemize

For each backend, the compiler generates:
@itemize
@item Stack operations translated to platform-specific register moves
@item Arithmetic translated to platform instruction sequences
@item Memory access translated to platform load/store instructions
@item Control flow translated to branch and jump instructions
@end itemize

@section Performance Considerations

@itemize
@item Word definitions enable tail-call optimization for recursive calls
@item Constant folding optimizes compile-time constant expressions
@item Dead code elimination removes unreachable word definitions
@item Common subexpression elimination optimizes repeated computations
@end itemize
